```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the RIP routing daemon (ripd) on router1, the sole
transit router connecting the router0 side (172.16.x, 172.20.x, 192.168.1.0)
with the router2 side (192.168.100.0, 10.50.x, 192.168.200.x) of the topology.

The "change" on router1 removed its FRR configuration: /etc/frr/frr.conf was
deleted entirely and /etc/frr/ripd.conf was wiped to 0 bytes. As a result,
ripd failed to start (it is absent from the process table; only zebra, mgmtd,
and staticd are running). Router1 has no RIP process active, no "router rip"
block in its running config, and its own routing table contains only two
directly-connected routes (eth2: 10.16.1.2/24, eth3: 10.20.1.1/24) plus two
static kernel routes — no RIP-learned paths at all.

The mechanism of "partial" loss is RIP's 180-second invalidation timer. Router1
sent its last RIP update at lab startup (~147 seconds ago). Both router0 and
router2 still hold those stale routes with only ~33 seconds remaining before
expiry (confirmed via "show ip rip" on both neighbours). Once those timers
hit zero, all inter-zone transit routes through router1 will be purged and
reachability between the two sides of the network will be completely severed.

Root cause: /etc/frr/frr.conf was deleted (and ripd.conf zeroed), preventing
ripd from starting and removing router1 from RIP participation entirely.

Corrective fix: Restore the RIP configuration on router1. Create or repopulate
/etc/frr/frr.conf (or /etc/frr/ripd.conf) with a valid RIP stanza — at minimum:
  router rip
    network 10.0.0.0/8
    version 2
  exit
Ensure the file is owned by frr:frr with mode 640, then run
"service frr restart" (or "systemctl restart frr") so ripd starts and
re-establishes adjacencies with router0 and router2.
=== END ===
```